 Data provenance was similarly uneven: for roughly a quarter of the submissions, the dataset in use was not evident from the project page or repository alone, so the per-project summaries in the preceding section state each project's data source explicitly wherever it is not obvious. The patterns are few. Some projects retrospectively queried published experimental datasets, for example the direct arylation yields of Shields et al.~\cite{shields2021bayesian} in project 15 and the Suzuki-Miyaura screen of Perera et al.~\cite{perera2018platform} in project 40. Others generated objective values on demand from physics-based simulators or synthetic test functions (projects 6, 36, 37, and 45), collected new measurements in-house (projects 30 and 31), or, for proposal-stage submissions, used no data at all (projects 19, 29, and 42). Even where a published dataset was used, it was more often fetched at run time or referenced from within the code than deposited in the repository: project 15, for instance, pulls its benchmark directly from the EDBO+ repository~\cite{torres_multi-objective_2022}. Archiving a copy of the queried dataset alongside the project code, with its provenance stated in the README, would make such outputs considerably easier to audit and reuse.
 
